course: space
lesson: 5
title: The Earth as a planet
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Our Earth is the third planet from the Sun, a rocky ball like Mercury, Venus and Mars, but the biggest of the four and the only one we know with life.
explain: It spins once a day, which gives us day and night, and it races round the Sun once a year at about 30 kilometres every second, though we never feel it, just as you do not feel a smooth train.
explain: The Earth leans over by about 23.4 degrees, and this tilt, not our distance from the Sun, makes summer and winter: in summer your half leans towards the Sun.
explain: Inside, it is like a peach: a thin crust of rock, a thick hot mantle, and an iron core whose swirling liquid part makes a magnetic shield against the solar wind.
explain: Seen from Voyager 1 six billion kilometres away, the whole Earth was a pale blue dot smaller than a single pixel.
fact: The Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the largest of the four rocky planets.
fact: The Earth is the only place in the universe where we know life exists, and the only planet known to have liquid water oceans on its surface.
fact: The Earth's axis is tilted by about 23.4 degrees, and this tilt, not our distance from the Sun, causes the seasons.
fact: The Earth spins once in about 24 hours, giving day and night, and goes round the Sun in about 365.25 days.
fact: The extra quarter of a day each year is why we add a leap day, 29 February, about every four years.
fact: The Earth has a thin rocky crust, a thick hot mantle, a liquid iron outer core and a solid iron inner core.
fact: Swirling liquid iron in the outer core makes the Earth's magnetic field, which shields us from the solar wind; without it, the solar wind would slowly wear away our air over a very long time.
fact: The Earth's air is about 78 percent nitrogen and 21 percent oxygen, and the oxygen was made by living things such as tiny ocean microbes and plants.
fact: The Earth is the only planet known to have plate tectonics: the crust is broken into plates that slowly move.
fact: The Earth bulges slightly at the equator, being about 43 kilometres wider across the equator than from pole to pole.
fact: The Earth races round the Sun at about 30 kilometres per second, about 107,000 kilometres per hour.
fact: In 1990 Voyager 1 photographed the Earth from about 6 billion kilometres away, and it appeared as a tiny pale blue dot.
exercise: Which planet from the Sun is the Earth? | The third | The first | The second | The fourth
exercise: Which is the largest of the four rocky planets? | The Earth | Venus, its twin | Mars | Mercury
exercise: What makes the Earth special among all known places? | It is the only place where we know life exists | It is the biggest planet in the whole Solar System | It is the only planet in the Solar System with a moon | It is the closest of all the planets to the hot Sun
exercise: What causes the seasons? | The tilt of the Earth's axis | The Earth getting closer to and further from the Sun | The Sun getting hotter and cooler | The Moon blocking sunlight in winter
exercise: By how much is the Earth's axis tilted? | About 23.4 degrees | About 90 degrees | About 2 degrees | It is not tilted at all
exercise: What gives us day and night? | The Earth spinning once in about 24 hours | The Sun going round and round the Earth each day | The Moon moving across and covering up the Sun | The Earth going round the Sun once every day
exercise: How long does the Earth take to go round the Sun? | About 365.25 days | Exactly 360 whole days | About 24 hours | About 29.5 days
exercise: Why do we have a leap day about every four years? | The year is about a quarter of a day longer than 365 days | The Earth slows down every four years | The Moon's orbit gets longer | Calendars were printed wrong long ago, and nobody ever fixed them
exercise: Which list is in order from the outside to the centre of the Earth? | Crust, mantle, outer core, inner core | Mantle, crust, inner core, outer core | Core, crust, mantle, ocean | Crust, inner core, mantle, outer core
exercise: Which part of the Earth is liquid iron? | The outer core | The crust | The inner core | The mantle's top layer
exercise: What makes the Earth's magnetic field? | Swirling liquid iron in the outer core | A giant magnet buried at the North Pole | The pull of the Moon | Electricity from lightning
exercise: What does the magnetic field protect us from? | Most of the solar wind | The pull of the Moon's gravity | Rain, hail and big storms | Earthquakes and volcanoes
exercise: What is the Earth's air mostly made of? | About 78 percent nitrogen and 21 percent oxygen | Mostly oxygen, with just a little nitrogen mixed in | Mostly carbon dioxide | Mostly hydrogen and helium
exercise: Where did the oxygen in our air come from? | Living things made it | It came out of volcanoes | It came from the Sun | It was always there from the start
exercise: What is plate tectonics? | The crust broken into plates that slowly move | The Earth spinning round and round on its tilted axis | Thick layers of ice that slowly move at the poles | The bulge of the Earth around the middle at the equator
exercise: How much wider is the Earth across the equator than from pole to pole? | About 43 kilometres | About 4,300 kilometres | Not at all, it is a perfect ball | About 43 centimetres
exercise: How fast does the Earth go round the Sun? | About 30 kilometres per second | About 30 kilometres per hour | About 3 metres per second | It does not move at all, the Sun moves
exercise: What did Voyager 1 photograph in 1990? | The Earth as a pale blue dot from 6 billion kilometres | The far side of the Moon, for the very first time ever, up close | The first close-up of Mars | The rings of Jupiter
test: my cousin reckons it's summer cos we're closer to the Sun then. right? | No, summer is when our half of the Earth leans towards the Sun | Yes, distance is the whole reason | Yes, and winter is when the Sun cools down | No, summer is caused by the Moon
test: In fact the Earth is closest to the Sun in early January. What does that tell a person in Britain? | Distance cannot be what makes the seasons, since January is winter there | The Sun must be cooler in January | Britain has summer in January | The Earth stops tilting in winter
test: If the Earth had no tilt at all, what would happen to the seasons? | They would almost disappear, every day of the year would be much the same | Summer would last all year everywhere | Winter would last all year everywhere | The seasons would swap round
test: We are zooming round the Sun at 30 km a second, so why don't we feel it? | We move smoothly together with the air, like on a smooth train | We are far too heavy to feel it, so the speed cannot push us about | It is only true at the equator | The Moon holds us steady
test: What would happen if the Earth's magnetic field disappeared? | More of the solar wind would reach the atmosphere and wear it away over time | The Earth would stop spinning at once | We would float off the ground | The Moon would fall down
test: A cartoon shows the inside of the Earth as hollow with a little sun in the middle. What is really there? | A solid iron inner core inside a liquid iron outer core | An ocean of water | Empty space with dinosaurs | A huge ball of ice
test: bro says the Earth's air was always full of oxygen since day one. true? | No, living things made the oxygen over a very long time | Yes, oxygen came with the Earth from the start, out of the rocks | Yes, the Sun breathes it out | No, the oxygen came from the Moon
test: Which planet is known to have moving plates of crust? | Only the Earth, as far as we know | Every rocky planet, including Venus and Mars | Only Mars | Only the giant planets
test: Is the Earth a perfect ball? | Almost, but it bulges slightly at the equator | Yes, perfectly round in every direction | No, it is flat like a plate | No, it is shaped like an egg pointing at the Sun
test: The Pale Blue Dot photo shows the Earth as a tiny speck. What does it show about the Earth? | How small our whole world is in the vastness of space | That the Earth is the biggest planet | That the Earth is shaped like a dot | That the Earth is the only blue planet in the whole universe
test: 2028 has a 29 February. Why don't we just have 365 days every year? | The Earth takes about 365 and a quarter days to orbit, so the extra quarters add up | 29 February is a holiday that was added for fun | The Moon's phases need an extra day | Leap days stop the Earth from tilting
exam: Identify the cause of the seasonal variation experienced upon the Earth. | The inclination of the Earth's axis of rotation | The variation in the distance of the Earth from the Sun | The periodic variation in the output of the Sun | The interposition of the Moon between the Earth and the Sun
exam: Ascertain the origin of the Earth's magnetic field. | The motion of liquid iron within the outer core | A permanent magnet situated at the geographic North Pole | The gravitational influence of the Moon | Electrical discharges within the atmosphere
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the composition of the Earth's atmosphere? | Approximately seventy-eight per cent nitrogen and twenty-one per cent oxygen | Predominantly carbon dioxide with traces of nitrogen | Predominantly oxygen with traces of nitrogen | Predominantly hydrogen and helium
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Earth among the rocky planets. | It is the largest of the four and the only one known to support life | It is the smallest of the four rocky planets | It is the rocky planet nearest the Sun | It is one of several rocky planets known to support life
